Explore the code to understand how it works today and what has to change. You can read files and run read-only commands (ls, cat, rg, find). You cannot change the repositories at this stage; only docs/ is writable.

Write the specification to docs/spec.md. A developer who has never seen the requirement must be able to implement it from the spec alone.

    # <title>
    ## Summary
    ## Target repositories      which repositories must change, and why; leave out the ones that don't
    ## Changes                  per repository: the files and modules to touch, and what changes in each
    ## Data and API changes     if any
    ## New dependencies         every package to add, or "none" — the development run may only install what is listed here
    ## Test plan                tests to add or update, and how to check the change by hand
    ## Acceptance criteria      AC-1, AC-2, …: each one verifiable from the diff or by running the tests; together they cover every R-n of the requirement
    ## Merge order              only when the pull requests cannot all merge at once; see below
    ## Risks and rollout

Some changes across repositories cannot merge at once: a shared module must be merged before the repositories that use it can pin its merged commit, or a service must be deployed before the clients that need it. Then write a "Merge order" section. Say which repositories merge first, what the next ones wait for, and what must change in them once that is so. They can wait for the earlier ones to be merged; released, meaning CI and deploys succeeded on their merge commits; or tagged, meaning a tag contains their merge commits, for dependencies consumed by version. A change could be "pin github.com/acme/api to the commit it merged as". When the change is approved for merging, tfy's merge run follows this section: it merges in that order, makes those changes, and has them reviewed. During development, a repository can already depend on another one's commit on the unit's branch, because the workspace serves its checkouts as dependencies.

Then return the structured output: target_repos (directory names from the list above), acceptance_criteria as {id, text} pairs matching the spec, new_dependencies, and a one-paragraph summary.
